%S A393538 1,1,1,1,2,1,1,3,3,1,1,5,4,5,1,1,8,27,27,8,1,1,13,295,40,295,13,1,1,
%T A393538 21,5168,3321,3321,5168,21,1,1,34,147719,24932515,4720,24932515,
%U A393538 147719,34,1
%N A393538 Numerators of the reduced fractions in the reciprocal-addition Pascal triangle, read by rows.
%C A393538 The first inward diagonal is A(n,1)=F(n+1)/F(n), so N(n,1)=F(n+1) and D(n,1)=F(n); this is the Fibonacci hook relating the array to A000045.
%C A393538 Let R(n,k)=N(n,k)/D(n,k) in lowest terms, with R(0,0)=1, edge values 1, and R(n+1,k)=1/R(n,k-1)+1/R(n,k). The sequence flattens N(n,k) in row-major order, m=n(n+1)/2+k.
%C A393538 Companion denominator sequence: A397827. This array is not the entrywise reciprocal of Pascal's triangle.
%H A393538 Naveed Javeed, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22057245">A Reciprocal-Addition Pascal Triangle: Continued Fractions and Diagonal Limits</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A393538 Naveed Javeed, <a href="https://github.com/najaweed/reciprocal-pascal">Source, exact tests and Lean formalization</a>.
%F A393538 N(n,0)=N(n,n)=D(n,0)=D(n,n)=1.
%F A393538 For 1<=k<=n, put X=D(n,k-1)*N(n,k)+D(n,k)*N(n,k-1), Y=N(n,k-1)*N(n,k), and g=gcd(X,Y). Then N(n+1,k)=X/g and D(n+1,k)=Y/g.
%e A393538 The first rows of R are [1], [1,1], [1,2,1], [1,3/2,3/2,1], [1,5/3,4/3,5/3,1]; hence the numerator rows are [1], [1,1], [1,2,1], [1,3,3,1], [1,5,4,5,1].
%o A393538 (Python)
%o A393538 from fractions import Fraction
%o A393538 def rows(m):
%o A393538     row = [Fraction(1)]
%o A393538     for _ in range(m + 1):
%o A393538         yield row
%o A393538         inv = [1/x for x in row]
%o A393538         row = [inv[0]] + [inv[k-1] + inv[k] for k in range(1, len(row))] + [inv[-1]]
%o A393538 print([x.numerator for row in rows(8) for x in row])
%Y A393538 Cf. A000045 (Fibonacci numbers), A007318 (Pascal triangle read by rows), and companion denominator sequence A397827.
%K A393538 nonn,tabl,frac,new
%O A393538 0,5
%A A393538 _Naveed Javeed_, Aug 24 2026
